text:
On Earth, telescopes have a problem: the atmosphere. The air moves and blurs the light from the stars; that is why they twinkle. The solution? Put a telescope above the atmosphere!

On 24 April 1990, the Shuttle Discovery carried the Hubble Space Telescope, as big as a bus, and put it into orbit about 540 kilometres up. It is named after the astronomer Edwin Hubble, who discovered that the [Universe] is expanding: galaxies are moving away from each other.

But disaster: the first pictures were blurry! The telescope's big mirror had been polished wrongly. The mistake was tiny, about one-fiftieth of the thickness of a hair, but it was enough to spoil everything. Newspapers made fun of Hubble.

In 1993, astronauts went up to fix it. They fitted a kind of "glasses" to correct the telescope's eyesight. After five spacewalks, the new pictures were stunning! Hubble was repaired and upgraded five times by Shuttle crews.

Hubble has made many discoveries:
- it helped measure the age of the Universe: about 13.8 billion years;
- it photographed very distant galaxies whose light set off more than 13 billion years ago;
- it showed that almost all galaxies have a huge [black hole] at their centre;
- it photographed clouds of gas where new stars are born, like the famous "Pillars of Creation".

More than 35 years after its launch, Hubble is still working!

The next big dream of space exploration is to send people to Mars. Several space agencies and companies are working on it. But it's a huge challenge, much harder than going to the Moon.

Here are some of the problems to solve:
- distance: the Moon is 3 days away, Mars about 6 to 9 months! You need to take enough food, water and air, or know how to recycle them;
- the return trip: Mars and the Earth are only well placed for travel once every 26 months. So a complete mission could last more than two and a half years;
- [radiation]: in space, far from Earth's magnetic shield, astronauts receive rays that are dangerous for their health;
- landing: Mars's atmosphere is too thin to slow you down well, but thick enough to heat you up;
- living there: it is about -60 °C on average, the air is not breathable, and there are huge dust storms;
- isolation: a message takes up to 20 minutes to get from Mars to Earth. You can't call for help live!

Scientists are looking for solutions: using Martian ice to make water and oxygen (the Perseverance rover has already made a little oxygen from Martian air!), growing plants in greenhouses, building shelters underground...

Volunteers already live for months in isolated bases on Earth, in the desert or in Antarctica, to test what life would be like for a Mars crew.

Maybe the first people to walk on Mars are children your age today!

People often think space is only for astronauts. Yet you use space every day without knowing it!

More than 10,000 working satellites are circling the Earth. They help us to:
- find our way: GPS in a car or phone uses signals from satellites; Europe has its own system, Galileo;
- forecast the weather and watch storms and hurricanes;
- watch TV, make phone calls and use the Internet everywhere;
- keep an eye on the climate: melting ice, rising seas, forests being cut down, fires. Satellites are the "eyes" that show us how our planet is changing;
- help after disasters like earthquakes and floods.

Many inventions from space exploration have also come into our lives: space blankets, water filters, camera sensors, better medical equipment...

But space has a problem: there is more and more [space debris]. These are old broken satellites, pieces of rockets and fragments from collisions. They travel very fast: even a small bolt can hit as hard as a bullet! The Space Station sometimes has to move to dodge them. We must learn to "clean up" space and stop polluting it.

Space exploration began as a competition between two countries. Today, it brings together women and men from all over the world.

It also reminds us that the Earth, seen from up there, is a small blue planet, beautiful and fragile. And you, would you like to become an astronaut, an engineer or a space scientist?
